\f0\fs34 \cf2 \strokec2 That is a fantastic project idea. Combining traditional, highly accurate GIS data (like LiDAR and parcel maps) with the cutting-edge photorealism of 3D Gaussian Splatting is exactly where the mapping industry is heading.\
Here is a step-by-step breakdown of how you could fuse the state's data with the techniques from the video to create a comprehensive, highly detailed 3D model of a home:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs40 \cf2 1. Define the Boundaries (Parcel Info)\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs34 \cf2 First, you need your "cookie cutter." You would download the homeowner parcel data (usually available as a shapefile or GeoJSON from county or state records) and load it into a GIS mapping software like QGIS (which is free) or Esri ArcGIS (which was mentioned in the video). This gives you the exact geospatial coordinates and legal boundary lines of the property.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs40 \cf2 2. Lay the Geometric Foundation (LiDAR Data)\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs34 \cf2 Next, you would go to the Utah Geospatial Resource Center (UGRC) portal and download the LiDAR point cloud files (usually in 
\f3\fs22\fsmilli11250 \cf6 \cb3 \strokec6 .las
\f0\fs34 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  or 
\f3\fs22\fsmilli11250 \cf6 \cb3 \strokec6 .laz
\f0\fs34 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  formats) for that specific parcel.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf2 Why this is valuable:
\f0\b0  State-level LiDAR is typically captured by airplanes. It bounces lasers off the ground to measure exact distances. This gives you incredibly precise, bare-earth elevation data, accurate roof pitches, and the exact heights of surrounding trees.\

\f2\b The limitation:
\f0\b0  Because it's captured from high above, aerial LiDAR is often monochromatic or low-resolution in color, and it misses ground-level details like the textures of the home's siding, windows, or front porch.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs40 \cf2 3. Capture the High-Fidelity Textures (The Video's Method)\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs34 \cf2 To fill in the visual gaps left by the aerial LiDAR, you need ground-level photorealism. This is where you use the tech from the video.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Take your smartphone (using an app like Niantic's Scaniverse) or a consumer drone (like a DJI Mini).\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Walk or fly around the specific home, capturing overlapping video or photos. You want to make sure you capture the sides of the house, the textures of the brick or wood, and the ground-level perspective.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs40 \cf2 \strokec2 4. Process via 3D Gaussian Splatting\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs34 \cf2 Upload your phone or drone footage to a cloud-based 3D Gaussian Splatting pipeline (like Luma AI, Polycam, or the other processing pipelines mentioned in the video). The AI will process the 2D images and reconstruct them into a beautifully textured, photorealistic 3D "splat" of the home.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs40 \cf2 5. Merge and Align\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs34 \cf2 Now, you have two assets: a highly accurate but visually dull LiDAR point cloud, and a beautifully photorealistic but potentially less geospatially accurate Gaussian Splat.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Using platforms like Cesium or Esri ArcGIS (both highlighted in the video as early adopters of this tech), you can import both datasets.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 You then georeference (align) your photorealistic Gaussian Splat so that it perfectly sits on top of the highly accurate LiDAR elevation data, all neatly contained within the property's parcel boundary lines.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs40 \cf2 \strokec2 6. Export as a glTF (The "JPEG Moment")\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs34 \cf2 Finally, because of the new standards discussed in the video, you can export this merged masterpiece as a single 
\f3\fs22\fsmilli11250 \cf6 \cb3 \strokec6 .glTF
\f0\fs34 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  file. You now have a complete, highly accurate, photo-real 3D image of the home that can be embedded on a website, viewed in a VR headset, or explored in AR through your phone.\
Are you looking to do this for a personal project, like mapping your own home, or are you thinking about a commercial application like real estate or construction?\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

