\f0\fs34 \cf2 \cb3 \strokec2 We want to help the homeowner visualize an ADU in the backyard. We will not have prior access to the parcel to accomplish step 3 as stated. Because the information in the video is so new, can you a web search for other methods for Salt Lake City?\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 \cb4 \strokec5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \cb1 \
\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs34 \cf2 \strokec2 It is completely understandable that you wouldn't have site access during the early conceptual phases. Fortunately, because Salt Lake City has excellent open data portals and the 3D mapping industry has shifted toward open streaming standards (as highlighted in the video), you have a few powerful, remote-first options to visualize an ADU.\
Here are three methods you can use to build this visualization without ever stepping foot on the property:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs40 \cf2 1. The "Google Earth" Method: Photorealistic 3D Tiles (Recommended)\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs34 \cf2 The video prominently featured a platform called 
\f2\b Cesium
\f0\b0 , which is designed to stream massive 3D environments on the web. Recently, Google opened up its entire high-resolution 3D Earth database to developers via 
\f2\b Photorealistic 3D Tiles
\f0\b0 .\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0
\f2\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 How it works:
\f0\b0  Salt Lake City is already fully mapped by Google's aerial photogrammetry (the same 3D mesh you see when you zoom into Google Earth). You can pull this exact 3D mesh into a web viewer using CesiumJS, or into software like Blender or Unreal Engine.\
\ls5\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Visualizing the ADU:
\f0\b0  Once the Salt Lake City 3D tile is loaded, you simply import the 3D model of your ADU (e.g., a 
\f3\fs22\fsmilli11250 \cf6 \cb3 \strokec6 .glTF
\f0\fs34 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  file) and drop it right into the property's backyard.\
\ls5\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Result:
\f0\b0  You get a highly realistic, textured 3D view of the neighborhood. The homeowner can see exactly how the ADU looks next to their existing roofline and surrounding trees, all from their web browser.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs40 \cf2 \strokec2 2. The Procedural GIS Method: UGRC Building Footprints & LiDAR\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs34 \cf2 If you need strict, centimeter-level accuracy for planning purposes rather than just pretty visuals, you can rely purely on state and county data.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0
\f2\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 How it works:
\f0\b0  The Utah Geospatial Resource Center (UGRC) and the Salt Lake County Surveyor provide highly accurate building footprints (often partnered with Microsoft's AI generation) and aerial LiDAR.\
\ls6\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Visualizing the ADU:
\f0\b0  You can load the LiDAR point cloud into a 3D viewer to get the exact terrain slope and tree heights. Then, use the building footprints to extrude the existing house to its true height. Finally, drop your ADU model into this environment.\
\ls6\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Result:
\f0\b0  It will look more like an architectural or engineering model (less photorealistic, more "pointillism" or blocky), but it is perfect for calculating exact slope, drainage, and shadow casting.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs40 \cf2 \strokec2 3. The Compliance Overlay: SLC Open Data Portal\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs34 \cf2 Visualizing the ADU is only half the battle; it also needs to be legal.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0
\f2\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 How it works:
\f0\b0  Salt Lake City has a robust GIS Open Data Portal. You can download the specific parcel lines, utility easements (like water and sewer lines), and zoning setback requirements.\
\ls7\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Visualizing the ADU:
\f0\b0  By bringing these 2D boundary layers into your 3D environment, you create a "safe zone" box in the backyard. You can visually prove to the homeowner that the proposed ADU physically fits within the legal boundaries, avoiding utility lines and maintaining the required distance from the fence.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec2 Using Google's 3D tiles combined with SLC's open parcel data is the fastest way to get that "wow" factor for a homeowner without needing to fly a drone.\
Are you currently working with pre-designed, modular ADU models, or will you be designing a custom 3D model for this specific backyard?\
