\f0\fs34 \cf2 \strokec2 Building a neighborhood-scale digital twin to showcase modular ADUs is a brilliant application of this technology. It perfectly aligns with the video's concept of blending massive, macro-level datasets with highly localized, micro-level scans.\
However, a quick reality check on using pre-existing photos and videos: 3D Gaussian Splatting requires a very specific type of visual data. A handful of random, disconnected photos of a backyard will fail to stitch together. The AI needs to see how the geometry changes from multiple overlapping angles. The good news is that if your videos are continuous walkthroughs, they are an absolute goldmine for this process.\
Here is how you can fuse homeowner-provided media, public GIS data, and your modular ADU models to create a localized digital twin.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs40 \cf2 Phase 1: Convert the Homeowner's Video into a Splat\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs34 \cf2 You can use the homeowner's video to generate the micro-level reality of their specific backyard, bypassing the need for a site visit.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0
\f2\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Extract and Process:
\f0\b0  Upload the continuous video to a Gaussian Splatting platform (like Luma AI, Polycam, or Postshot). The software will extract the frames, track the camera movement, and generate a 3D Gaussian Splat of the backyard.\
\ls8\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Clean and Crop:
\f0\b0  Use an editing tool (like PlayCanvas or SuperSplat) to crop out any messy artifacts or the homeowner's actual house if you only want to focus on the yard space.\
\ls8\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Export:
\f0\b0  Export this localized backyard scene as a standardized 
\f3\fs22\fsmilli11250 \cf6 \cb3 \strokec6 .glTF
\f0\fs34 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  file (the "JPEG moment" format).\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs40 \cf2 \strokec2 Phase 2: Establish the Neighborhood Context\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs34 \cf2 To make it a true digital twin, the homeowner needs to see their yard within the context of their actual Salt Lake City neighborhood.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0
\f2\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Load the Macro Map:
\f0\b0  Use a 3D geospatial platform like CesiumJS to load Google's Photorealistic 3D Tiles for Salt Lake City. This acts as your base layer, providing the surrounding streets, neighboring houses, and distant mountains.\
\ls9\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Align the Data:
\f0\b0  Import the localized 
\f3\fs22\fsmilli11250 \cf6 \cb3 \strokec6 .glTF
\f0\fs34 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  backyard splat you created in Phase 1 and georeference it so it perfectly overlays the corresponding parcel on the Google 3D Tile base.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs40 \cf2 \strokec2 Phase 3: Insert the Modular ADU\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs34 \cf2 Because your ADUs are pre-designed, this is the easiest part of the workflow.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0
\f2\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Format the ADU:
\f0\b0  Ensure your modular ADU 3D CAD models are exported as standard 
\f3\fs22\fsmilli11250 \cf6 \cb3 \strokec6 .glTF
\f0\fs34 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  or 
\f3\fs22\fsmilli11250 \cf6 \cb3 \strokec6 .glb
\f0\fs34 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  files.\
\ls10\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Place and Scale:
\f0\b0  Drag and drop the modular ADU file directly into the localized backyard splat. Because both files use real-world scales, the ADU will accurately reflect how much space it will consume next to the homeowner's existing fences and trees.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

