\f0\fs34 \cf2 \strokec2 Using Jumpstart Pro with Rails 8 is a highly pragmatic choice. It gives you all the boilerplate\'97authentication, database setup, and background workers\'97so you can focus entirely on the complex logic of merging geospatial data with 3D models.\
Architecting this in Rails means you are essentially building a bridge between heavy backend data processing and a lightweight, interactive Javascript frontend. Here is how you can structure those two datasets and present them seamlessly to your users.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f5\fs48 \cf2 \strokec2 Data Set 1: The Parcel Digital Twin (The Context)\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs34 \cf2 \strokec2 This dataset is dynamic, user-generated, and requires heavy lifting. Your Rails app will need to collect raw data, communicate with external APIs to process it, and store the resulting 3D assets.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls11\ilvl0
\f2\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Geospatial Storage:
\f0\b0  Use PostgreSQL with the 
\f2\b PostGIS
\f0\b0  extension. This allows you to store the homeowner's parcel boundaries, utility easements, and exact latitude/longitude coordinates natively in your database, making it easy to query if an ADU legally fits the space.\
\ls11\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Media Collection:
\f0\b0  Use 
\f2\b Active Storage
\f0\b0  to handle the homeowner's uploaded videos of their backyard. Since these video files will be large, configure Active Storage to upload directly to an S3 bucket (or equivalent) to avoid tying up your Rails server.\
\ls11\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Background Processing:
\f0\b0  Use Rails 8\'92s default background job backend, 
\f2\b Solid Queue
\f0\b0 . When a video finishes uploading, trigger a background job to send the video file via API to a 3D Gaussian Splatting service (like Luma AI or Polycam).\
\ls11\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Asset Retrieval:
\f0\b0  Have your background job listen for a webhook from the 3D service. Once the AI has processed the video into a 3D model, your app will automatically download the resulting 
\f3\fs22\fsmilli11250 \cf6 \cb3 \strokec6 .glTF
\f0\fs34 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  file and attach it to the Parcel record via Active Storage.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f5\fs48 \cf2 Data Set 2: The Modular ADU (The Product)\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs34 \cf2 \strokec2 This dataset is static, relational, and acts as your product catalog.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls12\ilvl0
\f2\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Relational Data:
\f0\b0  Standard Rails models will handle this perfectly. You will have tables for the ADU models containing their exact dimensions, square footage, pricing, and structural specifications.\
\ls12\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Constraint Data:
\f0\b0  Include data fields for local zoning rules\'97such as required setbacks from the fence line or maximum height limits. Your Rails app can run a validation to check if the ADU dimensions conflict with the PostGIS parcel boundaries from Data Set 1.\
\ls12\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 3D Assets:
\f0\b0  Store the pre-designed, standardized 
\f3\fs22\fsmilli11250 \cf6 \cb3 \strokec6 .glTF
\f0\fs34 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  3D models of your modular ADUs in Active Storage, linked to their respective ADU database records.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f5\fs48 \cf2 The Presentation Layer (Frontend Fusion)\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs34 \cf2 \strokec2 Rails does not render 3D graphics natively, so you will need to leverage the Javascript ecosystem via Hotwire, specifically using 
\f2\b Stimulus
\f0\b0 .\
To present the digital twin to the homeowner, you will build a dedicated view where Data Set 1 and Data Set 2 visually merge.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls13\ilvl0
\f2\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The 3D Engine:
\f0\b0  You will need to bring a Javascript 3D library into your Rails asset pipeline. 
\f2\b Three.js
\f0\b0  is the industry standard for rendering 
\f3\fs22\fsmilli11250 \cf6 \cb3 \strokec6 .glTF
\f0\fs34 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  files, though libraries like 
\f2\b CesiumJS
\f0\b0  are better if you want to include the Google Maps 3D tiles mentioned in the video.\
\ls13\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Stimulus Controller:
\f0\b0  Create a Stimulus controller (e.g., 
\f3\fs22\fsmilli11250 \cf6 \cb3 \strokec6 digital_twin_controller.js
\f0\fs34 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2 ) to act as the bridge.\
\ls13\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Passing the Data:
\f0\b0  In your Rails view (ERB or HTML), you will pass the Active Storage URLs of both the Parcel 
\f3\fs22\fsmilli11250 \cf6 \cb3 \strokec6 .glTF
\f0\fs34 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  and the ADU 
\f3\fs22\fsmilli11250 \cf6 \cb3 \strokec6 .glTF
\f0\fs34 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  to the Stimulus controller using data attributes.\
\ls13\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Final Render:
\f0\b0  The Javascript takes those two URLs, loads both 3D models into the same digital scene, and allows the homeowner to drag, drop, and rotate the ADU within their digitally recreated backyard.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
